PASS level_intents: all 120 levels have a specific intent
PASS no_filler_levels: no trivial filler levels after the tutorial
FAIL level_design: level-design review 7.4/10 (bar 8), 0 level(s) below 6/10: This is a well-structured, readable puzzle set. It has clear one-idea-per-level teaching, a consistent teach/practise/twist/boss/send-off cycle, and a visible forecast that keeps failure fair. The weak spots are repetition and a few pacing problems. First, "heavy pair shares a line" and "leave one stop out" appear roughly a dozen times with only the skin changed. Second, the breathers are near-clones of each other. Third, the combine cities stack rules too fast, and casual win rates drop to the low 50s. Several levels also show suspiciously small skilled-over-casual gains (L62, L71, L120), which points to fiddly execution rather than insight. Individual levels are mostly 7-9. The set is solid but not yet at Royal Match or Flow Free polish. It needs about 8-12 levels swapped or retuned and a variety pass on the aha types. Curve: Strong spine, with one ladder that repeats. Each city introduces one mechanic alone (tunnel L13, carriage L25, hill L37, ferry L49, snow L61, market L74, interchange L73, loop L85, wave L97, heritage L109). It then practises it, adds a twist, and ends with a boss and a gentle mastery send-off. Breathers sit after bosses (L7, L19, L31, L43, L55, L67, L79, L91, L103, L115). Late-third casual win rates (about 50-70%, averaging roughly 60%) hit the target, and every verified-fair boss sweep shows trap rate 0.

Problems:
1. Every city runs the same cycle, so by city 5 the rhythm is predictable. Each has a teach level, then "the obvious move is the trap," then a boss with a late station, then a mastery level. The aha types mostly recycle: "split the line at the heavy stop," "heavy pair shares one line," "leave one out," and "late station wants a free line end." These are the same insight in different costumes. The strongest levels (L46, L82, L99, L105) bring a genuinely new angle.
2. Too many "tidy network is the trap" tells. Players learn to distrust the first plan they draw. Add decoys where the tidy plan is correct.
3. Breathers L7, L43, L67 and L91 are nearly one template: a ring or T, a dead-end stop, and a "draw one line" puzzle. They are pleasant but low-pressure repeats.
4. The mechanic ladder is clean until the combination cities (L52-60, L113-120). There, 3-4 rules arrive at once and casual win rates fall to 50-58%. Combining cities need an easier first combine and more distinct signature shapes.
5. Skilled-versus-casual gaps are inconsistent. L35, L59 and L103 sit near 99/68, which suggests a readable-but-fiddly step. L62 (73/77), L71 (65/80) and L120 (55/67) show skill barely helping, which means a hidden fiddly step. Check these for guess-based difficulty.
6. Rule-introduction load spikes at L100-120. Heritage, loops, one-way rails, tunnels, ferries, waves and cards all return in the same few levels. Spread them out or lead with a recap level.
7. The "?" trap rates on L70, L76, L77, L78, L100, L103-107, L112, L120 mean the sweep was capped. Re-run those to confirm no single early fatal move before shipping.
REDESIGN THESE LEVELS (keep the ones not listed):
L22 (6/10): This is L9's 'serve the heavy crowd directly' idea and L5's 'carry on to the destination' idea in a new setting. The answer comes from reading which town sends 'twice as many' riders. A transfer-over-bridge with A---F is a mild twist but it is not a new insight. → Make the weight less obvious: balance demand so the west town is bigger only by counting transfers, or let the east town have the heritage-like constraint. Alternatively cut and replace it with a one-tunnel level where the late station is on the quiet side.
L43 (6/10): A near-clone of L7: the dead-end temple H is the only Lantern, and the answer is a 'pretty line' puzzle. B and E send 0 riders, so they are just clutter. Little new after L7. → Replace with a different texture, e.g. a tiny hill board where two valid routes differ only in how many hills they cross, and the 3rd star asks for the lower-hop route.
L62 (6/10): Casual 73% vs skilled 77% means skill barely helps. The level is about reading one number, then a fiddly execution (4 tracks, 1 spare). It also repeats L5's idea (carry the heavy stop's line on to its destination). → Loosen the execution: raise seats a bit or allow two valid layouts. Alternatively pair the snow with a different idea, since the mill idea is a repeat.
L103 (6/10): Casual 79% is fine, but the 3rd star ('A and F on one line') repeats L70/L106's heavy-pair idea in a lighter skin, so it is not fresh. → Swap for a breather that uses waves differently, e.g. a timed card or pre-building for the wave.
L106 (6/10): This is L9's heavy pair (here 16+16) with a hill rung. Trap rate shows '?', casual 52%. It is a rehash with extra rules. → Replace with a level where the express choice conflicts with the loop (e.g. the pair sits on the ring's far side) or merge into L70.
L115 (6/10): Eight tracks for a six-track answer makes it a walkover. The aha repeats the heavy-pair/hub idea. Casual 89% sits next to L114 (63%) and L116 (59%). → Cut to 7 tracks and tie the 3rd star to keeping one spare; add a wave cue so the forecast matters.
L7 (7/10): The 'F is a dead end so it must be a line end' puzzle is a trick of the one-path rule with no network thinking. It is also the template reused in L43, L67 and L91. Goal 35 of 50 with 2 lines makes the breather feel like it plays itself. → Keep L7 as the template. Retire the clone breathers or give each a different texture, e.g. L43 uses a timing gimmick and L91 uses a choice between two valid ways.
L9 (7/10): The 24/24 pair is so lopsided that the answer is visible from the demand cards. It is a good aha, but it is the first of many. Casual 73% at level 9 is a spike after L8 (83%) and L10 (75%). → Lower A/D to about 16 each, add some crossing traffic from B/E, so the choice is a judgement call. Reserve the 24+ heavy pair for the late repeats.
L12 (7/10): The 3rd star (every rider) needs the exact snake with transfer E, but the board says 'two tidy lines still win' and costs the star 20 riders. Casual 89% is high for a send-off with a twist, and the tidy trap is mild. → Raise the goal so that the tidy two-line plan needs the transfer to win, or add one more demand into J so the missing transfer fails outright. Keep it gentle but real.
L29 (7/10): Good reversal, but it sits right after four carriage-is-the-answer levels, so it is predictable. Nine tracks in week 1 with only 8 optimal moves feels loose. → Cut tracks to 7 so the 3rd star ('1 track to spare') depends on the split. Add a small decoy demand to G/H so the carriage looks tempting for a reason.
L35 (7/10): Casual 68% vs skilled 99% is a 31-point gap with a '?' search cap. The all-fair-sweep says all moves can win, but the carriage placement is fiddly. The week-3 H opening needs two tracks and two carriages in the same plan, which looks like a checklist. → Add a week-3 card or hint cue showing the forecast on H. Re-run the sweep without the cap to confirm no early fatal move.
L51 (7/10): The unusual all-cross demand makes the answer clear once you see it, but the '3rd star: use at most 2 lines' is the only thing separating it from the plain win. Casual 71% is fine. → Make the second-boat answer less symmetrical, e.g. unequal crossing counts, so players must work out which pair shares a boat.
L70 (7/10): This is 'put the heavy pair on one line' again (L9, L20) with a ladder-shaped grid. Casual 56% at 70 is on target, but the sweep shows '?', and the 3rd star needs exact lines. → Re-run the sweep. Reduce A/F to 14 each so that the two-halves plan fails less brutally, and make the crossing choice (B-E vs another rung) the real decision.
L71 (7/10): Casual 65% vs skilled 80%. The forced one-track choice is a binary, 'pick the correct line to extend,' and the demand numbers show which. Little room to get it wrong in an interesting way. → Add a card choice in week 3 (calm or extra track) so the boss tests reading the forecast instead of just the demand cards.
L78 (7/10): Casual 62% with 5 first moves and a '?' trap rate. Both cards work, so the decision is weak. The late one-hop E-H line is the only real step. → Make one card slightly worse (e.g. calm only works if H is a line end) so the card choice has real stakes.
L97 (7/10): Casual 89%, skilled 100%: the carriage is the only move, so it is a tutorial. Fine as a teach, but it follows L96 (65%), so the difficulty swing is large. → Move the teach earlier in the city or give it a spare decision (place the carriage on one of two lines).
L100 (7/10): Casual 49% is the hardest of the combined levels, and the goal of 544 of 640 leaves little room. The aha ('close both triangles') is obvious from the shape, so the difficulty comes from tight numbers and not insight. → Lower the goal to about 500, or add a decoy (a third bridging stop) so the aha has a real alternative.
L107 (7/10): Skilled 79% and casual 52% at the late boss is harsh. The kiosk-skip is the same idea as L89/L90/L94. Trap E full wk3 suggests an early fatal choice. → Remove one repeated element (e.g. the kiosks) and add a new one: a week-2 card or a choice about when to buy the carriage.
L112 (7/10): Casual 50% / skilled 83% is the lowest early heritage level. It also says the 'Meridian Shield boss follows,' but L114 follows L113, so the ordering note is wrong. The aha is the heavy-pair idea again. → Raise casual toward 60% by trimming the cross-hall demand, and fix the sequence (place it right before L114).
L113 (7/10): Both rings win; only the 3rd star depends on the choice. A weak decision with no failure, and the real fail ('no ring at all') is a tidy-line mistake rather than a puzzle. → Make one ring clearly worse in goals so players must read the heritage demand, or add a late station that fits only one ring.
L120 (7/10): Casual 55% / skilled 67% with the search capped: skill barely helps. The test is a checklist of earlier ideas, with the heavy T-A pair repeated again. A finale should feel climactic, but the numbers say punishing. → Lower the goal by about 15 to lift skilled to about 80%. Add one new beat (the T-A shuttle needs a small timing choice) so it is more than a recap.

